A||||6||GT Bastard||Anybody knife-edged a their crankshaft?||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||07-17-2000||01:22 PM||||I'm debating whether it's worth delaying my motor build-up to have the crank knife-edged.  Has anybody seen good results with this? thanks..||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phoenix||07-17-2000||02:38 PM||||I've never done this to L4 or V6 but I have done this to my 911 engine (flat 6 boxer,dry sump) along with several other Porsche-club friends. The main bearing journals and crank were "sharpened up" with a hand die grinder and a milling machine to remove as much of the square edges as possible without affecting any of the structural integrity or balance.  Since and engine is realy just a air pump, allowing the air to flow around the inside with less resistance can free up some hp. No dyno tests - but reliable sources claim about 4 to 5 hp on a 210 hp 911 engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||07-17-2000||06:59 PM||||That's high RPM and racing stuff. Not needed for a mid range street engine. Any power gains will be minimal compared to cost and the time needed to fit it correctly. I usually spend my time and money with making the engine flow better...works for me.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||07-17-2000||07:39 PM||||I agree with Batboy. Knife edging a crankshaft is for high rpm race motors.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||07-17-2000||08:11 PM||||Thanks, that's what I needed to know.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||07-17-2000||10:18 PM||||I'll bet a good crankshaft scraper/windage tray would give you the same power,and you wouldn't be grinding away on such a critical part of your engine for your 5 HP.<BR>Porsche motors are dry sumped too,great for cornering,but it takes 3 gallons of oil at change time!||||reg||1
Z||000006||PJ||07-18-2000||12:18 AM||||Speaking of windage trays... I was the fortunate recipient of a free '96? 3.1L warranty motor (scuffed piston) from my local Pontiac dealer. The windage tray it was equipped with fit nicely into my stock 2.8L oil pan. Just had to use the studded main bolts and beat on the oil pan a little bit for clearance. Cheap performance upgrade, even if you had to buy the parts.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black  ||||reg||1
